{
  "type": "doc",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "true_false_container",
      "attrs": {
        "id": "70317e0c-43ce-484b-b900-50c2ad5b9757",
        "class": "true-false"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "question_node_true_false",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "38c2696c-5d39-44b8-b9cb-bf5d913acd41",
            "class": "true-false-question"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Which of the following are True statements about mutations that could cause the wrong amino acid to be specified by a given codon in an organism (for example, “UUU” always results in GLY being added to a growing polypeptide rather than PHE)? ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "true_false",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "5d57779f-6395-4e42-ae4c-d4dccb8351f4",
            "class": "true-false-option",
            "answer": false,
            "correct": true
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A mutation occurs in the gene encoding an aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase, causing the wrong amino acid to fit into the amino acid binding pocket of that enzyme.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "feedback_prompt",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "54bcf91d-237d-4ced-b388-9bbbd3f50af1",
            "class": "feedback-prompt"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "When many genes all have the same mutation, it is not due to simultaneous mutation in unrelated genes, but to an underlying mechanism that is common among them all. A mutation in an aminoacyl-transferase gene could change the shape of the aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, changing which amino acid is added to a given tRNA by this enzyme.  Every time the anticodon of that tRNA recognizes an mRNA codon during translation, the wrong amino acid would be added to a growing polypeptide.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "true_false",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "cecc4bc3-08dc-45e2-947d-e68b98195c6c",
            "class": "true-false-option",
            "answer": false,
            "correct": true
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A mutation occurs in a tRNA gene, changing the sequence of that tRNA anticodon.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "feedback_prompt",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "fc3dba10-4e02-47b4-be90-63e2d14dcfe2",
            "class": "feedback-prompt"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "When many genes all have the same mutation, it is not due to simultaneous mutation in unrelated genes, but to an underlying mechanism that is common among them all. A mutation in a tRNA gene that changed the sequence of the anticodon would result in that tRNA-aa recognizing a different mRNA codon throughout the organism. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "true_false",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "56f3ddea-61bf-46d5-8193-23c69b421146",
            "class": "true-false-option",
            "answer": false,
            "correct": false
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "An environmental chemical mutagen causes a mutation of every codon in the cellular genome for that specific amino acid.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "feedback_prompt",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "7e393d76-2f59-4ab2-8a47-5bf9e944a302",
            "class": "feedback-prompt"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Mutations occur at random. A chemical mutagen would not cause a simultaneous, identical mutation in every gene in the organism.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "true_false",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "46b52605-f7ca-4d72-ad5e-98ed6c736d8d",
            "class": "true-false-option",
            "answer": false,
            "correct": false
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A mutation in a DNA repair gene results in errors in replication remaining uncorrected throughout the organism.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "feedback_prompt",
          "attrs": {
            "id": "54f14f47-8f35-40e7-91fa-a17f32eafa05",
            "class": "feedback-prompt"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "attrs": { "class": "paragraph" },
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Mutations in DNA repair genes that result in nonfunctional DNA repair enzymes will increase the number of mutations that go uncorrected in an organsim's genome. This can lead to nonfunctional gene products or changes in feedback regulation, but this would not change which amino acid is specified by a given codon during translation.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "attrs": { "id": "", "group": "", "track": [], "viewid": "" },
